{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Word onsets evoke a suppression of mu‐band (8–12 Hz) power relative to pre‐onset baseline across MEG sensors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify mu‐band power desynchronization (suppression) following word onsets relative to baseline.",  
      "steps":"1. For each session (1–10), load the MEG data matrix and corresponding events.tsv. 2. Select rows where kind=='word' and extract their timemeg values and durations. 3. For each word onset, extract epochs of MEG data from –0.2 s to +0.5 s around timemeg. 4. Apply an 8–12 Hz bandpass filter to each epoch. 5. Compute instantaneous power (Hilbert envelope squared) for each epoch and channel. 6. Define baseline window (–0.2 to 0 s) and post‐onset window (0 to 0.3 s). Compute mean power in each window per channel and event. 7. Perform paired t‐tests (post vs baseline) for each channel and correct p‐values for multiple comparisons (e.g., FDR).","deliverables":"1. Grand‐average mu‐power time courses (mean±SD) around word onsets. 2. Channel‐wise t‐statistics, raw and FDR‐corrected p‐values, and indicators of significant suppression. 3. Topographic map of sensors showing significant mu‐band suppression."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Phoneme‐evoked beta‐band (13–30 Hz) power increases differ in magnitude between magnetometer and gradiometer sensors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare post‐onset beta‐power effect sizes between magnetometers and gradiometers.",  
      "steps":"1. Load MEG data and phoneme events for all sessions. 2. Extract –0.2 to +0.5 s epochs around each phoneme onset. 3. Bandpass filter epochs in 13–30 Hz and compute power. 4. For each channel, compute the effect size per event as (mean post‐onset power [0–0.3 s] minus baseline power [–0.2–0 s]). 5. Using the channel_types attribute, group effect sizes into magnetometer vs gradiometer pools. 6. Perform an independent‐samples t‐test comparing the two sensor types (across channels) on their average effect sizes. 7. Optionally, bootstrap group‐difference confidence intervals.","deliverables":"1. Distributions (histograms or boxplots) of beta‐power effect sizes for mag vs grad sensors. 2. Statistical test results (t‐stat, p‐value, CI). 3. Bar plot showing mean effect size ± SEM by sensor type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The spatial pattern of beta‐power increase evoked by phoneme onsets is reproducible across the ten recording sessions.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify inter‐session consistency of channel‐wise beta‐power responses to phoneme onsets.",  
      "steps":"1. For each session, compute for each channel the mean post‐onset (0–0.3 s) beta power minus baseline (–0.2–0 s). 2. Assemble a matrix of size (sessions × channels) of these effect sizes. 3. Compute the pairwise Pearson correlation between sessions across channels, resulting in a 10×10 correlation matrix. 4. Optionally compute intraclass correlation (ICC) across sessions for each channel to quantify reliability. 5. Test whether average correlation is significantly greater than zero via permutation test.","deliverables":"1. Session‐by‐session correlation heatmap of beta‐effect maps. 2. ICC values per channel and summary statistics. 3. Permutation‐test p‐value for overall inter‐session consistency."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The latency to peak beta‐band power following phoneme onset is shorter for last phonemes in a word than for first phonemes.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the timing of maximal beta‐power response at first vs last phonemes within words.",  
      "steps":"1. Load events.tsv and identify phoneme rows. 2. Group by sentenceidx and wordidx to determine, for each word, the maximum phonemeidx (last phoneme). 3. Create two sets of onsets: first phonemes (phonemeidx==1) and last phonemes. 4. Extract –0.2 to +0.5 s epochs around each onset and bandpass filter in 13–30 Hz. 5. Compute time‐resolved power per epoch and channel. 6. For each event, find the latency of the maximum power in 0–0.3 s. 7. For each channel, perform a paired t‐test on latencies (last vs first phonemes) and correct for multiple comparisons.","deliverables":"1. Distributions of peak latencies for first vs last phonemes. 2. Channel‐wise t‐statistics and corrected p‐values. 3. Summary plot (e.g., raincloud) showing latency shift."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Phoneme duration positively correlates with the magnitude of beta‐band power increase at onset.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the trial‐by‐trial relationship between phoneme duration and beta‐power response magnitude.",  
      "steps":"1. Load phoneme events with their duration from each session’s events.tsv. 2. Extract –0.2 to +0.5 s epochs around each onset, bandpass filter in 13–30 Hz, compute baseline and post‐onset mean power. 3. For each event and channel, compute the effect (post‐onset minus baseline). 4. For each channel, compute the Pearson correlation between event durations and their effect sizes. 5. Test the significance of each correlation (two‐tailed t‐test) and correct across channels (FDR).","deliverables":"1. Topographic map of correlation coefficients. 2. Raw and corrected p‐values per channel. 3. Example scatter plot (duration vs effect) for a representative channel."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The magnitude of beta‐power increase at phoneme onset decreases for later words within each sentence.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for a linear trend of phonemic beta response as a function of word position in the sentence.",  
      "steps":"1. From events.tsv, select word‐level segments and use their sentenceidx and wordidx to index word position. 2. Extract –0.2 to +0.5 s beta‐band epochs around each word onset and compute event‐wise effect sizes (post minus baseline). 3. Fit a linear regression of effect size versus wordidx for each channel. 4. Extract slope coefficients and their t‐statistics. 5. Test whether slopes differ from zero (p‐values) and correct across channels.","deliverables":"1. Distribution of regression slopes across channels. 2. Channel‐wise slope t‐stats and corrected p‐values. 3. Scatter plot of effect size vs word index with regression line for a sample channel."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Phoneme onsets evoke significant theta‐band (4–8 Hz) phase locking across trials (intertrial phase coherence) in focal sensor regions.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compute and test event‐related theta‐phase consistency (ITPC) at phoneme onsets.",  
      "steps":"1. Extract –0.2 to +0.5 s epochs around phoneme onsets for each session. 2. Bandpass filter each epoch in 4–8 Hz. 3. Compute analytic phase via Hilbert transform. 4. For each timepoint and channel, compute ITPC = abs(mean(exp(i·phase), across events)). 5. Define baseline ITPC (–0.2 to 0 s) and post‐onset ITPC (0 to 0.3 s) and test their difference via nonparametric permutation test per channel/timepoint. 6. Correct for multiple comparisons using cluster‐based or FDR methods.","deliverables":"1. Time‐resolved ITPC curves for representative channels. 2. Statistical significance map (channels × time) of ITPC increase. 3. Topographic map of channel clusters showing significant theta‐phase locking."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Pre‐stimulus mu‐band (8–12 Hz) power negatively correlates with phoneme‐evoked beta‐band response magnitude on a trial‐by‐trial basis.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether higher baseline mu power predicts smaller post‐onset beta increases.",  
      "steps":"1. For each phoneme event, extract –0.2 to +0.5 s epochs. 2. Bandpass filter each epoch separately in mu (8–12 Hz) and beta (13–30 Hz). 3. Compute mean mu‐power in the baseline window (–0.2 to 0 s) and beta‐power effect (post‐onset mean [0–0.3 s] minus baseline) per event and channel. 4. For each channel, compute Pearson correlation across events between baseline mu‐power and beta‐effect size. 5. Test correlation significance and correct across channels.","deliverables":"1. Topographic map of correlation coefficients (mu vs beta response). 2. Raw and FDR‐corrected p‐values per channel. 3. Example scatter plot for a sensor showing the negative correlation."  
    }  
  }  
]}
